\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}

\begin{document}

\section*{Definition of Compact Operator}

An operator \( K: X \to Y \) is said to be \emph{compact} if for every bounded set \( B \subset X \), the image \( K(B) \) is precompact in \( Y \). This means that the closure of \( K(B) \) is compact in \( Y \).

\section*{Example}

Consider the operator \( K: \ell^2 \to \ell^2 \) defined by:
\[
K(x_1, x_2, x_3, \ldots) = \left( \frac{x_1}{12}, \frac{x_2}{24}, \frac{x_3}{36}, \ldots \right).
